  "rendered_text": "The actual mover remains unidentified. I found no primary job or access record establishing that UMS or Max Movers moved Zim or worked at WTC 1, 2 or 7. This is “not found,” not evidence that a job could not have occurred.\n\nThe five-page brief includes a job register, exact source links and page numbers, and six ranked record targets with likely custodians.\n\nWhat the evidence establishes\nThe move was planned months ahead. American Shipper announced the September relocation on April 3. Contemporary reporting identifies Hunter Management as the owner’s representative, but does not name the physical carrier. \nFreightWaves\n+1\nThe August 10 fax documents insurance correspondence. I visually verified both company names. UMS supplied carrier identifiers for itself and Max to DeBellis Insurance. The fax identifies no Zim customer, WTC address, moving job or subcontract. Section 2, PDF p. 13; duplicate p. 32. \nmanuals.plus\nCommon ownership is a separate witness claim. An employee told the FBI that UMS handled local moves and Max long-distance moves, sharing an owner and premises. That statement—not the fax—supports reported common operation. Philadelphia Serial 2, p. 4. \nisraellobby.org\n\nThe highest-value next targets are Zim’s vendor/payment records and Hunter’s move file, followed by dock/elevator approvals and billing for 1 WTC, suite 1601. The tenant release supplies that precise suite locator; the FBI insurance interview separately identifies a 1A attachment associated with NK1765. \ncryptome.org\n+1\n\nNo custodians were contacted or requests submitted. A confirmed moving job alone would not establish explosives placement.\n\nZim_Mover_and_WTC_Access_Research.pdf\nPDF",
